                <pb facs="0929" n="805" sameAs="frus:frus1881/pg805"/>

                <head>No. 476.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hitt to Mr. Morgan.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1881-09-15"><hi rendition="#italic">September</hi> 15,
                        1881</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 174.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt of your
                    dispatch No. 260, of the 25th ultimo, reporting that in an interview with the
                    Mexican minister for foreign affairs it was ascertained that there was, in the
                    present instance, no likelihood of an extension of the “zona libre,” and to
                    express the satisfaction with which this intelligence has been received. Without
                    manifesting too great an interest in the subject, you might say, in any informal
                    conversation with Mr. Mariscal, that the assurances contained in your dispatch
                    relative to “zona libre” had been received with evident satisfaction by your
                    government.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>ROBERT R. HITT,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Acting
                        Secretary.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
